course: biology
lesson: 306
title: Beetles: the biggest group of animals on Earth, wing cases and six legs
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: About 400,000 kinds of beetle are known, more than any other group of animals, and there may be a million more still not named. Roughly one animal kind in four is a beetle.
explain: Watch a ladybird before it flies: its red shell splits down the middle and lifts, and thin wings unfold from underneath like origami. The hard shell is really a pair of front wings turned into cases, called elytra, and they are the secret of beetles: armour that keeps the flying wings safe.
explain: Beetles have six legs like every insect, biting jaws that open sideways, and a life in four stages: egg, grub, pupa and adult. Many spend years as a fat grub in soil or wood and only weeks as an adult.
explain: You can find them almost anywhere: in a pond carrying a bubble of air, in a bag of flour, in a rotten log, even in a museum cleaning skeletons. The tiniest are smaller than a full stop; the biggest are longer than your hand.
fact: About 400,000 kinds of beetle are known, more than any other animal group, and there may be more than a million kinds still not named; the scientist J. B. S. Haldane is said to have joked that the creator had «an inordinate fondness for beetles», which means he must love beetles far more than anything else.
fact: A beetle's hard front wings are called elytra; they cover the soft flying wings, lift up in flight, and the thin back wings fold like origami to fit underneath.
fact: Many beetles have five segments on each foot, others four or three, and each foot ends in two tiny claws; scientists count the segments to sort beetles into families.
fact: Beetles have biting jaws that open sideways, not up and down like ours, and can cut leaves, wood, other insects or dung.
fact: Beetles change completely: egg, larva, pupa, adult; the cockchafer grub lives underground about three or four years, while the adult lives only a few weeks.
fact: Beetles live almost everywhere: ponds, deserts, mountains, caves and kitchens; flour beetles can live in a packet of dried food, and the great diving beetle carries a bubble of air under its wing cases to breathe underwater.
fact: Featherwing beetles are among the smallest, some about a third of a millimetre long, with feathery wings like little brushes; the titan beetle of South America can be about 16 centimetres long.
fact: Museums use hide beetles to eat dried flesh off bones without harming them, cleaning a small skull in a few days, but similar beetles are pests that eat stuffed animals and wool.
fact: Scientists keep the word «bug» for one group of insects with a straw-like mouth; beetles chew, true bugs suck, and a beetle's wing cases meet in a straight line down its back.
fact: A ladybird's spots do not show its age; they show its kind, so a seven-spot ladybird always has seven, and the bright colours warn birds that it tastes bad.
fact: The diabolical ironclad beetle of the USA has armour that scientists found could take a force about 39,000 times its weight; its wing cases are joined like jigsaw pieces and do not open, so it cannot fly.
fact: Like all insects, a beetle has six legs, three body parts and two feelers.
exercise: About how many kinds of beetle are known? | About 400,000 | About 4,000 | About 40 | About 40 million
exercise: What are elytra? | A beetle's hard front wings that work as cases | A beetle's long feelers that it uses to smell food | The claws at the end of its feet | The jaws of a beetle grub
exercise: What happens to a ladybird's wing cases when it flies? | They lift up and the thin back wings unfold | They fall off and grow back later in the summer | They flap fast like a bee's wings | They stay shut and it glides
exercise: How many claws are at the end of each beetle foot? | Two | One | Five | None
exercise: Why do scientists count the segments in a beetle's foot? | To sort beetles into families | To work out the beetle's age | To guess how fast it runs | To know how many eggs it lays
exercise: How do a beetle's jaws open? | Sideways | Up and down like ours | Only forwards | They never open
exercise: What are the four stages in a beetle's life? | Egg, larva, pupa, adult | Egg, tadpole, froglet, adult | Seed, shoot, bud, flower | Egg, chick, nymph, adult
exercise: How long does a cockchafer grub live underground? | About three or four years | About three days | About thirty years | It never lives underground
exercise: How does a great diving beetle breathe underwater? | It carries a bubble of air under its wing cases | It has gills along its sides, just like a fish | It does not need any air | It sips air through a long tube from the shore
exercise: About how long are the smallest featherwing beetles? | About a third of a millimetre | About 3 centimetres, as long as a thumb | About 1 metre | About 3 millimetres
exercise: Why would a museum both love and fear hide beetles? | They clean skeletons but similar beetles eat stuffed animals and wool | They guard the doors at night but bite the visitors who come in by day | They glow at night but scare guards | They clean windows but break glass
exercise: How can you tell a beetle from a true bug? | A beetle chews and its wing cases meet in a straight line | A beetle has eight legs and a bug has only six legs | A beetle sucks juice through a straw and a bug chews | There is no difference at all
exercise: What do a ladybird's spots tell you? | Its kind, not its age | Its age, one spot per year | How many eggs it has laid | How many times it has flown
exercise: Why does the diabolical ironclad beetle not fly? | Its wing cases are locked together like jigsaw pieces | It is too heavy to lift off with its huge jaws | It has no legs to take off with | It is afraid of heights
exercise: How strong is the armour of the diabolical ironclad beetle? | It took a force about 39,000 times its weight | It breaks if you blow on it gently | It is as soft as a grub | It is made of real iron from the rocks it eats
exercise: Where can flour beetles live? | In a packet of dried food | Only in deep ponds | Only in snowy caves high up mountains | Only inside living trees
test: Count them: 4 ladybirds sit on a leaf. How many legs and how many feelers? | 24 legs and 8 feelers | 16 legs and 4 feelers | 32 legs and 8 feelers | 24 legs and 4 feelers
test: Count them: a beetle has five segments on each foot. How many foot segments on all its feet? | 30 | 20 | 15 | 10
test: Count them: a seven-spot ladybird gets a month older. How many spots does it have now? | Still 7 | 8 | 14 | 6
test: In A Bug's Life, Francis the ladybird and Dim the beetle are called «bugs». What would a scientist say? | Beetles and ladybirds chew, so they are not true bugs, which suck through straws | Every insect is a true bug | Ladybirds are true bugs, because they are small and round, but beetles are not | Only spiders are true bugs
test: «gross there's a fat white worm in the soil» You see it is curled and has six little legs near its head. What is it most likely? | A beetle grub, perhaps a chafer | An earthworm that grew legs | A baby snake that has not grown up yet | A caterpillar's egg
test: What would happen if a museum's hide beetles escaped into the hall of stuffed animals? | They, or similar beetles, could eat the stuffed animals | They would clean all the glass cases until they shone | They would starve at once | Nothing, they only eat bones in labs
test: «why's that beetle look like it's holding a shiny ball underwater?» What is it? | A diving beetle carrying a bubble of air to breathe | A beetle blowing bubbles for fun to show off to others | A beetle laying a glass egg | A beetle with a cold
test: Why might beetles be so successful? | Hard wing cases protect them so they can live almost anywhere | They are the only animals with legs | They never have any enemies | They live for hundreds of years and lay millions of eggs each year
test: A shield bug looks just like a beetle. What would show it is a true bug? | Its straw-like mouth for sucking | Its six legs | Its two feelers | Its hard, shiny back like a shield
test: Three featherwing beetles in a row are about 1 millimetre long. About how many would fit in a line 1 centimetre long? | About 30 | About 3 | About 10 | About 300
test: «a car ran over that beetle and it just walked off?? no way» Which beetle could this be? | The diabolical ironclad beetle, with very strong armour | A ladybird, which is made of rubber | Any beetle, they are all unbreakable | A titan beetle, because it is the biggest and so the strongest
exam: Identify the structures designated elytra in beetles. | Hardened forewings that enclose and protect the membranous hind wings | Sensory appendages situated upon the head | Segments of the foot employed in classification | Mouthparts adapted for the sucking of fluids
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the number of described species of beetle? | Approximately four hundred thousand species are described, more than any other animal group | Approximately four thousand species are described, fewer than birds | Precisely one million species are described and no more remain | Beetles constitute a single species with numerous varieties
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the spots of the seven-spot ladybird. | The number of spots indicates the species and does not alter with age | The number of spots increases by one with each year of life | The number of spots decreases as the insect ages | The number of spots indicates the number of eggs laid
exam: Ascertain the principal distinction between beetles and the insects that scientists term true bugs. | Beetles possess biting jaws, whereas true bugs possess a sucking, straw-like mouth | Beetles possess eight legs, whereas true bugs possess six | Beetles lack wings, whereas true bugs possess four | Beetles inhabit only water, whereas true bugs inhabit only land
